**Definition 2.3.** Let \((S, \cdot)\) be a semigroup, let \(n \in \mathbb{N}\) and let \(A \subseteq S\). We say that

- \(A\) is a thick set if for any finite subset \(F \subset S\), there exists an element \(x \in S\) such that \(Fx = \{fx : f \in F\} \subset A\);
- \(A\) is a syndetic set if there exists a finite set \(F \subset S\) such that \(S = \bigcup_{x \in F} x^{-1}A\), where \(x^{-1}A = \{y : xy \in A\}\);
- \(A\) is piecewise syndetic if there exists a finite set \(F \subset S\) such that \(\bigcup_{x \in F} x^{-1}A\) is a thick set. It is well known that \(A\) is piecewise syndetic if and only if there exists \(p \in K(\beta S, \cdot)\) such that \(A \in p\).
- \(A\) is central if it belongs to a minimal idempotent in \(\beta S\).

It can be proved that a set \(A\) is thick iff there exists a left ideal \(L\) such that \(L \subseteq A\). And a set \(A\) is syndetic iff for every left ideal \(L\), \(L \cap A \ne \emptyset\). Note that if \(f : (S, \cdot) \to (T, \cdot)\) be any map between discrete semigroups. Then \(\tilde{f} : \beta S \to \beta T\) be the continuous extension of \(f\) defined by \(\tilde{f}(p) = \tilde{f}(p-\lim x) = p-\lim f(x)\), where \(p-\lim f(x) = y\) if and only if for every neighbourhood \(U\) of \(y\), \(\{x : f(x) \in U\} \in p\). For details about the theory of ultrafilters, we refer to the book [15].
